  - It's guidance, not a sandbox. Every write, edit, move and delete
    still asks you first, and your own instructions win when they
    conflict with this file.
-->

Pantry is a small web app for tracking what's in the kitchen: a
TypeScript + Express API in `server/`, a React front end in `web/`, and
SQLite for storage.

## Where things live

- `server/src/routes/` — one file per resource (`items.ts`, `shelves.ts`).
- `server/src/db/schema.sql` — the current schema. Migrations are in
  `server/src/db/migrations/`, numbered `0001_*.sql`, `0002_*.sql`, …
- `web/src/components/` — React components, one per file, PascalCase.
- `shared/types.ts` — types used by both sides. Change them here; never
  redefine them locally.

## Before you change anything

- Start with `search_files` or `find_files`, then `read_file`. Don't
  guess at what a file contains.
- Use `edit_file` for changes to an existing file. Use `write_file` only
  for new files or when replacing most of one.
- Keep changes to what I asked for. Don't reformat or tidy code you
  weren't asked to touch.

## Don't touch

- Existing migrations. Add a new one with the next number instead.
- `package-lock.json` and `web/dist/` — generated.
- `.env` — private. Don't read it, and don't quote it if you see it.
- `vendor/` — third-party code, copied in on purpose.

## Checking your work

You can't run commands. When a change is done, tell me what to run:

- `npm test` — unit tests (Vitest), from the repo root.
- `npm run lint` — ESLint and Prettier.
- `npm run dev` — starts the API and the web app on http://localhost:5173.

When you add a function with real logic in it, add a test beside it
(`foo.ts` → `foo.test.ts`).

## House style

- TypeScript strict mode; no `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow it.
- Named exports only.
- Dates are stored as ISO 8601 strings in UTC and formatted only in `web/`.
- User-facing text is British English ("colour", "organise").
- Comments explain why, not what.

## Words we use

- **Item** — a thing in the pantry. Has a quantity and an optional expiry.
- **Shelf** — where an item is kept (Fridge, Freezer, Cupboard). An item
  is on exactly one shelf.
- **Low stock** — quantity at or below the item's `threshold`. Not the
  same as empty.

## How to answer

- Lead with what you changed and why, in a sentence or two.
- If a request could mean two things, ask before editing files.
- Mention problems you noticed but didn't fix, rather than fixing them.

## This file

If something here turns out to be wrong or out of date, say so and offer
an edit to `LOACHFILE.md`.
